    {
      "id": "etQwmU4gGIZecA6Wbevr7",
      "type": "frame",
      "x": 3296,
      "y": 2988,
      "width": 1280,
      "height": 720,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bHC",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2037055623,
      "version": 4,
      "versionNonce": 1163400090,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1794794897624,
      "created": 1794794897624,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "customData": {
        "slidesOrder": 2,
        "mcpSlideLayout": "diagram",
        "mcpSlideDesign": {
          "style": "excalidraw",
          "accent": "violet",
          "decor": true
        },
        "presenterNotes": "- About 3 minutes. IA means Interaction Agent; EA means a saved Execution Agent with its own identity, assigned work and history. An EA run is one execution of that saved agent.\n- Use the illustrative request on the slide: draft Sarah a reminder for INV-1042. The IA retrieves candidates, inspects the exact invoice and current instructions, then reuses its owner rather than creating a second EA for the same work.\n- The persistent queue admits the job under global and per-EA bounds. The EA loads bounded history, retrieves original source detail when needed, and saves an email-tool draft. The IA shows the exact draft and awaits user approval before sending.\n- The registry, search vectors, queue and execution memory use SQLite. EAs call Gmail and Task tools; those external tool details are omitted from this overview.\n- Scheduled Triggers enter through the same queue and share its controls. Next, unpack discovery, context and execution one at a time."
      },
      "name": "03 — Architecture: one invoice request"
    },
