    {
      "id": "aIAPs9w4j1K6uO8I-AQK4",
      "type": "frame",
      "x": 14706,
      "y": 2988,
      "width": 1280,
      "height": 720,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bQz",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1690695207,
      "version": 10,
      "versionNonce": 232727238,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1794797333590,
      "created": 1794794897957,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "customData": {
        "slidesOrder": 7,
        "mcpSlideLayout": "table",
        "mcpSlideDesign": {
          "style": "excalidraw",
          "accent": "violet",
          "decor": true
        },
        "presenterNotes": "- About 2 minutes. These choices solve different growth problems: discovery, memory and execution.\n- Exact identifiers and conflicting invoices use production identity guards. Hybrid combines lexical and semantic rankings; semantic-only tied hybrid on this corpus, so the result supports the mechanism without proving hybrid is always superior.\n- A recent-only window loses old corrections and approval state. A summary preserves continuity; original source records supply exact evidence when needed.\n- A semaphore caps concurrent work in one process. The persistent queue also records waiting work, supports restart recovery, groups outcomes and serializes runs for each saved EA.\n- The 8,000/2,000 estimated history/summary budgets reserve room for current work. The 100-running/100-waiting defaults are configurable admission policy, validated by a burst simulation rather than claimed as optimal production capacity."
      },
      "name": "08 — Why these choices?"
    },
